# TideLM profile: GLM-5.3, exlr8 bit322 experts (3.22 bpw), two Sparks (TP2), served by the Rust
# engine (rust/crates/tidelm-glm, scripts/rust-up.sh). The exlr8 profile served since 2026-11-18, from
# the release's 0.7 (every expert re-encoded in the mul1 codebook in 0.6; this file was the `home`
# profile's until then, whose manifest 0.6 removed). It holds 11,543 experts resident (60.1%) and
# decodes prose 20.35 / code 25.36 tok/s, 8K first token 17.1 s (results/rust-glm53-exlr8-bit322-20261118;
# home: 20.38 / 23.91, 17.2 s). Held-out NLL at fixed residents follows residency, not the release's KL:
# 1.907 here against bit260's 1.855 with 2,800 more experts resident (docs/exlr8.md, `bit322`). bit340
# (10,946 residents) was served for an hour first and dropped for its answers.
# Start: cp configs/glm53-exlr8-bit322.env.example .env.glm53-exlr8-bit322, edit the paths, then: scripts/tide.sh start .env.glm53-exlr8-bit322    Stop: scripts/tide.sh stop
# README for the numbers this profile gives. Only what the Rust engine reads is here:
# scripts/rust-up.sh maps each GLM_* key to its flag, with its default, and refuses the choices it
# does not implement rather than serve something else. Measurements marked Python were taken on
# the Python engine (TIDE_ENGINE=python, its settings in configs/glm53-exl3.env.example as of
# a6ec917), at the settings this one runs.

PEER=you@10.0.0.2
# The non-expert weights, config and tokenizer, at the same path on both nodes: bertholomus's EXL3
# 3.0bpw checkpoint without its routed experts (15 GB, tools/glm_fetch_dense.py) with exlr8's 4-bit
# matrices in place of its 5-6-bit ones - o_proj, q_b, the dense MLPs of layers 0-2 and the shared
# experts - written by tools/glm_exlr8_dense.py (README) from the same release as the pack (the shared
# experts are mul1 since 0.6, the dense matrices still mcg). What exlr8 keeps at 8 bits (q_a, kv_a,
# kv_b, the indexer, the embedding, the head) stays the EXL3 checkpoint's: fewer bytes a pass.
GLM_MODEL_DIR=/home/you/models/GLM-5.3-dense-exlr8
PORT=8000
SERVED_MODEL_NAME=glm-5.3
# API_KEY: the key clients must send, as OpenAI's do (Authorization: Bearer <key>) or Anthropic's
# (x-api-key: <key>); several, comma-separated. Unset, the server answers anyone. /health stays open.
# API_KEY=change-me

# Context. The MLA latent cache in FP8, split across the ranks: 256K is 7.3 GB a rank. The FP4/FP8
# mix (Python's GLM_KV=fp4) fit 320K in less, at KL +0.004 on the long eval, but in use the model's
# recall of its context was visibly worse (2026-10-20); this engine serves FP8.
# 327,680 positions (320K) is the README's rows and the author's serving value: 9.1 GB a rank,
# 1.8 GB more than 256K (262144), which GLM_SLOTS below already leaves room for.
GLM_MAX_SEQ=327680
# FP8 KV cache: +0.002-0.005 NLL over BF16 and half its memory (~900 EXL3 slots at 128K).
GLM_KV=fp8
# Each rank caches every other position: half the KV memory, decode at parity.
GLM_KV_SPLIT=1

# Expert memory per rank, in 3-bit experts' worth (7.1 MB each: 88 GB at 12,400) - a budget, not a
# count. bit322's records are 2/2/3 to 4/4/4 bits by layer block, so 12,400 holds 11,543 experts (60.1%
# of the decoder layers' routed experts; by width 2,399 2/2/3, 3,675 3/3/3, 1,543 3/3/4, 770 4/4/3,
# 3,156 4/4/4) where home held 11,430 and bit340 10,946. The widths are pools of slots sized at boot
# from the routing history: a swap or a hot read replaces a resident of its own width, so the split
# moves only at a restart. At 12,400 with 320K of context and two lanes the pair serves at ~7 / 9 GiB
# MemAvailable (rank 0 / 1); rank 0 binds (it runs the HTTP server); each 1 GB is ~140 slots.
# Quality follows residency here: at fixed residents held-out NLL went 1.855 (bit260, 14,133
# residents) -> 1.879 (EXL3 2.75, 12,980) -> 1.907 (bit322, 11,359); speed does not (taking 3,200
# residents away moved tokens a round by -1.2 +- 1.1%, docs/residency-speed.md, EXL3).
GLM_SLOTS=12400
# Routed experts: a profile (manifest) of kindlingai/glm-5.3-exlr8-k2-k3-k4, fetched by
# tools/exlr8_fetch.py and packed per rank by tools/glm_pack_exlr8.py (README). bit322 gives each
# layer block one width a projection - gate and up 2 bits in layers 3-17 and 4 in 48-72, down 4 in
# 38-67, 3 elsewhere - and the MTP layer's experts 3. The pack has the EXL3 pack's layout
# (GLM_EXPERT_FORMAT stays exl3) and the release's codebook, mul1 rounded to FP8 E4M3 ("mul1-e4m3",
# which the engine reads from the pack). GLM_EXL3_PACK's /packs is GLM_EXL3_DIR. Another profile is
# another pack from the same mirror, and this key.
GLM_EXPERT_FORMAT=exl3
GLM_EXL3_DIR=/home/you/models/glm-exlr8-packs
GLM_EXL3_PACK=/packs/glm-exlr8-bit322-r{rank}of2.bin
# exlr8's codebook rounds every value to FP8 E4M3. fast (the default): the rounding in two
# instructions - 845 of the 65,536 mul1 states (1,078 of mcg's) decode to a neighbouring E4M3 value; a verify
# pass 1.7-2.0% shorter, held-out NLL -0.0002 +- 0.0042 (3,477 tokens, the same boot). exact: the
# format's own (nearest even). In the boot guard and the disk prefix store's namespace.
# GLM_EXLR8_ROUND=exact
# The checkpoint publishes MLA's absorbed kv_b in FP16; it is quantized at load to FP8 with
# Quark's 128x128 blocks: decode's absorbs 72-83 -> 43-45 us a layer, 0.58 GB a rank less
# (docs/glm53-native-exl3.md). The INT8 embedding is converted from this checkpoint's own.
GLM_KVB_FP8=1
GLM_EMBED_8BIT=int8

# Prefill. 4,096-row chunks: 8K prompt 18.9 -> 17.8 s (Python) for ~1 GB more transient memory
# during a prefill (2026-10-20); 2,048 holds that GB back.
GLM_CHUNK=4096
# A prefill chunk's attention partials summed over the ranks in BF16 instead of fp32 (or fp32):
# 8K prompt 0.75 s less attention time (Python), one BF16 step on each layer's attention output;
# against the fp32 sum, every expert exact, long set: KL 0.014 / top-1 97.3%, the same as a
# one-bit control (2026-10-20).
GLM_PREFILL_REDUCE=bf16
# Prefill a new message of 4-512 tokens twice, loading what it routes to in between: Japanese
# turns against every expert exact, EXL3 KL 0.096 -> 0.074 and 0.110 -> 0.084 (a new topic on a
# cached conversation), +1-3 s to the first token.
GLM_PREFILL_TWO_PASS=512
# ... unless the first pass computed less than this share of the new tokens' routed weight on
# substitutes. Measured 2026-10-20 at 12,000 EXL3 slots: fresh topics missed 27-41% in their first
# pass, follow-up turns on a settled conversation 12-14%, and the second pass costs 1-3 s of first
# token; its quality gain was measured at the fresh-topic miss rates, not at 12-14%.
GLM_PREFILL_TWO_PASS_MISS=0.15
# Prompt prefixes on disk: another chat or Open WebUI's title/tag requests no longer cost a long
# conversation's next turn a full prefill (GB a node).
GLM_PREFIX_DISK=1
GLM_PREFIX_DISK_GB=60

# MTP speculative decoding (GLM_MTP=0: off), up to GLM_MTP_DEPTH drafts a round.
GLM_MTP=1
GLM_MTP_DEPTH=7
# The chain stops on the calibrated acceptance: a table of measured acceptance by draft position
# and stated chain probability, threshold at a verify row's break-even (the MTP's own product is
# optimistic 2-3x at depth). Python, bench.py 2026-10-23, same prompts, one boot an arm: code 20.24
# tok/s at 3.42 drafts a round against the flat 0.9 gate's 19.60, prose 15.31 against 15.10 (the
# code gain is the size of the noise). Per-position thresholds on the stated probability
# (0.7,0.5,0.2,0.1,0.05) measured worse on the same run: 17.86 / 13.29, the rows they add worth
# 4-17 tok/s in an 18-15 tok/s round. GLM_MTP_CAL=0 runs the flat gate, GLM_MTP_DYN.
GLM_MTP_CAL=0.36
GLM_MTP_DYN=0.9
# Copy rounds: once the last 8 tokens repeat earlier text in the prompt or answer, a round verifies
# that text's continuation instead of MTP drafts: same rows, no draft passes, exact at any
# temperature. Python, one boot, interleaved, greedy: rewriting a 200-line file 27.8 -> 33.8 tok/s
# (6.9 of 7 copied drafts accepted), re-running a tool call 25.3 -> 33.2, prose 13.3 -> 14.2 with no
# copy rounds (the drift, ~+6%, is in all three).
GLM_LOOKUP=8
# Long copies (the DS engine's copy drafter): once the answer's last 6 tokens occur in the prompt,
# a round verifies up to 32 tokens of what followed them there, the next copy sized from the last
# one's acceptance (2 x accepted + 2, 7-32), ahead of the lookup above; one pass of up to 33 rows.
# Rust, one boot, greedy, 600 tokens, off / on (2026-10-27): rewriting a file 36.5 / 36.9 -> 55.9 /
# 56.5 tok/s (7.7 -> 26 tokens a round), re-running a tool call 32.1 / 34.9 -> 39.3 / 39.7, quoting
# code, fresh code and prose unchanged. 0 leaves the lookup alone.
GLM_COPY=1

# Requests served at once (1 unless set). Two share the KV pool: GLM_MAX_SEQ positions in all, one
# request alone still reaching GLM_MAX_SEQ; a second waits until its prompt + 4,096 positions fit,
# and one the pool cannot grow ends at its length. A decode pass carries both requests' rows. Rust,
# one boot, 2026-10-27: one request at a time is byte for byte GLM_LANES unset;
# two at once give 13-33% more tokens a second in all, each at 11.5-14.5 tok/s, their answers
# coherent but diverging early from the one-at-a-time ones (the residents and the 16-row passes'
# numerics differ); +0.5 GB at boot. A new request takes the free lane whose sequence shares most
# of its prompt, else the one used longest ago, so a title request beside a conversation keeps
# that conversation's KV. On in the example (the README's rows, the author's serving value).
GLM_LANES=2
# Beside a decoding lane, a long prompt prefills in parts of this many rows with a round of the other
# lane between them (a 19.2K prompt: 58 s, the other lane a round every ~3 s); alone it prefills
# in GLM_CHUNK rows.
#GLM_LANE_CHUNK=1024

# Expert adaptation: always once after prefill (before the first decode step), then every
# GLM_ADAPT_EVERY generated tokens; swaps capped per adaptation. 512 after prefill: 2,048 cost
# 1.7 s more TTFT on a 3.3K prompt for the same decode miss (36.2% vs 36.3%).
GLM_ADAPT=1
GLM_ADAPT_EVERY=200
GLM_ADAPT_PREFILL_SWAPS=512
GLM_ADAPT_DECODE_SWAPS=256
# Session-demand preloading: a request that continues a conversation (a prefix-cache hit with
# a fresh suffix) loads its conversation's already-voiced demand before that suffix prefills
# -- the loads the boundary pass above would pay for after the prefill, paid before it, so
# the suffix computes on the real experts and the boundary pass plans less. The cap on
# experts a request may preload; 0 off. The votes are keyed by the conversation's opening
# and never leave the process. docs/session-preload.md; the serving effect is not measured.
# 512 matches the boundary pass's cap (GLM_ADAPT_PREFILL_SWAPS) and is what the author serves with.
GLM_PRELOAD=512
# Hot misses: per layer, the heaviest missed pick with router weight share >= 0.2 is read now,
# computed exactly, and kept resident over a cold, lowest-ranked victim (GLM_HOT_KEEP victims
# queued a period); at most 16 reads a step, paced evenly over the layers (by the i-th of n MoE
# layers, at most ceil(16 * (i+1)/n)). The threshold, cap and pacing are fixed at the best row of
# the Python measurement, Tokyo eval in 3-row (MTP depth 2) steps, KL to exact / top-1, tok/s:
#   target-4 controller, bg 8, not kept   0.299 / 86.4%   10.7
#   0.2, cap 10, first come, kept         0.235 / 88.7%    9.19
#   0.2, cap 10, paced, kept              0.212 / 89.9%    9.29
#   0.2, cap 12, paced, kept              0.189 / 89.2%    9.03
#   0.2, cap 16, paced, kept              0.171 / 90.9%    8.95
#   0.2, no cap, kept                     0.172 / 90.3%    8.24
#   0.15, cap 16, paced, kept             0.194 / 90.1%    -- (1.8x the reads; light misses churn residents)
#   0.25, cap 10, kept                    0.257 (one-row)  10.39
GLM_HOT=1
GLM_HOT_KEEP=32
# Async hot reads: the same reads planned after each decode pass and read into their victims'
# slots while the next round drafts, so the pass is one graph and waits for nothing; an expert
# then serves the rounds after the one that missed it. Rust, README protocol, one boot an arm
# (2026-10-27): prose 16.73 -> 17.64 tok/s, code 20.84 -> 22.24 (a round 128 -> 121 / 144 -> 135
# ms). Python measured the quality on the same design (2026-10-19): KL to exact 0.086 -> 0.137,
# top-1 94.0% -> 92.4%, about what 2,400 fewer residents cost. Needs GLM_BG=1. On in the example
# since 2026-11-13: the README's rows were measured with it, and it is how the author serves.
# 0: the sync reads above, every one in the pass that missed it, at the speed above.
GLM_HOT_ASYNC=1
# Inline rescue, beside async hot reads only (with GLM_HOT_ASYNC=0 the launcher does not pass it:
# the sync reads above already read a layer's heaviest miss in the pass that missed it). A decode
# pass runs as its segments, and a missed pick with router weight share >= GLM_HOT_RESCUE is read
# into the transient slot before its layer's MoE and computed exactly - the heavy first occurrence
# covered in the pass that missed it, not the one after (docs/hot-predict.md). On since 2026-11-13
# by the author's choice, from serving with it, not from a measurement; 0.3 since 2026-11-18 (0.4
# rescued ~0.15 picks a pass, 0.3 ~0.6-1.0, at the bench's speed above). On GLM-5.3 its NLL A/B has
# not been run (on GLM-5.3-Flash 0.4 cost +0.0048 NLL at an unchanged pass). 0: off.
GLM_HOT_RESCUE=0.3
# Background period: every 2 rounds rank 0 plans the victims kept hot reads replace. Nothing else
# is read in the background: next to kept hot reads, background reads cost speed for little (8
# reads, 0.2 uncapped, kept: 7.9 tok/s KL 0.152 vs none + cap 10: 9.19 tok/s KL 0.164, one-row
# eval), and prefill prefetch took a 3.3K prompt's prefill miss 33% -> 25% but TTFT 23.7 -> 32.8 s,
# decode miss unchanged.
GLM_BG=1
GLM_BG_EVERY=2
# The routing history behind the boot placement is the engine's own, saved after every
# adaptation; the first boot starts it from the bundled seed (profiles/glm53-experts-v2.npz: the
# development pair's routing through 2026-11-13, about 18M rows, see its .json).
#RUST_GLM_STATE_DB=/home/you/tidelm-rust-state/glm-stats-rust.npz
#RUST_GLM_PREFIX_DIR=/home/you/tidelm-rust-state/glm-prefix

# Answers. GLM's template knows low / high; anything else (the server's own default, medium)
# renders "Reasoning Effort: Max". Low keeps chat answers short. A request overrides it with
# reasoning.effort, reasoning_effort or chat_template_kwargs.reasoning_effort (first wins).
GLM_REASONING_EFFORT=low
# Reply cap when the request sends no max_tokens (Open WebUI doesn't); clamped to the context
# left, so in effect "until the context is full" (DS V4.1's default too). 4096 cut off HTML
# pages with their reasoning. The KV cache is sized by GLM_MAX_SEQ, not by this.
GLM_DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS=131072
# A cycle of 1-8 tokens about to repeat a 12th time: that token is excluded and the next one
# drawn again (substituted experts open repetition loops; the model holds them).
GLM_LOOP_BREAK=12
# Abliterated GLM-5.3 (README): Blackfrost's DERISKED edit as one direction a layer projected out
# of layers 2-49's outputs; the file from tools/glm_ablit_fit.py, at this host path on both nodes.
# Unset (the default) serves the stock model.
# GLM_ABLIT=/home/you/models/glm53-ablit/glm53-derisked-l2-49.safetensors
